How to Manage Your Period at School or College

Getting your period at school or college can sometimes feel stressful, especially if you are worried about leaks, cramps, or not having a sanitary pad available. With some preparation, managing menstruation away from home can become much easier.

One of the best habits is to keep a small period emergency kit in your school or college bag. It can include a few sanitary pads, spare underwear, tissues, and a small disposal bag.

If your menstrual cycle is reasonably predictable, tracking your period can help you know when you may need extra supplies. However, periods can be unpredictable, particularly during the early years of menstruation.

Carry more than one pad, even if you think your period may be ending. Unexpected spotting or a heavier day can happen.

Choosing the right pad for your flow is also important. If you experience heavy bleeding, carry a higher-absorbency option rather than relying only on a small pad.

Change your sanitary pad regularly during the day. School and college bathrooms may not always be ideal, so keeping your supplies organised in a small pouch can make changing easier.

Wash your hands before and after changing your pad whenever possible. Used pads should be wrapped properly and placed in a suitable bin.

If you experience period cramps during classes, simple comfort measures such as taking a short break, gentle movement, hydration, or using an appropriate pain-relief method may help, depending on your individual situation.

It is also helpful to know where the nearest bathroom is. If your school or college has a nurse, counsellor, or designated support person, you can ask for help if you need menstrual supplies or medical attention.

Keeping an extra pair of underwear in your bag can also provide peace of mind in case of an unexpected leak.

